    "# Inside the first 490 steps — parameters and gradients side by side\n",
    "\n",
    "`evolution.ipynb` looks at 1000 epochs at epoch resolution. By the end of epoch 1 the matrices\n",
    "have already grown ~60% in norm, so that view cannot show what happens at initialization.\n",
    "This notebook uses `inquiring_at_init.py`, which re-runs the identical setup for 5 epochs and\n",
    "snapshots $W_{qk}$ after **every optimizer step**.\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "25,000 train sequences / batch 256 = 98 steps per epoch\n",
    "5 epochs x 98 = 490 steps  ->  491 rows, row 0 = the random init before any step\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**The two runs are the same trajectory.** The training script cross-checks its epoch-boundary\n",
    "snapshots (steps 98/196/294/392/490) against rows 1..5 of the 1000-epoch run and records the\n",
    "result in `metrics_*.json`; it came back `max|diff| = 0.0` for both layers at all three\n",
    "thresholds. So everything below is literally the opening of the long run, not a re-run that\n",
    "merely resembles it.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is plotted\n",
    "\n",
    "Four panels in one frame, per step:\n",
    "\n",
    "| | |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| $W_{qk}$ layer 1 (108x108) | $W_{qk}$ layer 2 (216x216) |\n",
    "| $\\partial \\mathcal{L}/\\partial W_{qk}^{L1}$ | $\\partial \\mathcal{L}/\\partial W_{qk}^{L2}$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "with the batch loss and the norms tracked underneath and the current step marked.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The gradients were not stored during training**, so they are recomputed. Two versions:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **`minibatch`** (default) — the *exact* gradient the optimizer used at that step: parameters\n",
    "  $W(s)$ evaluated on the same batch $s$ that produced the step $W(s)\\to W(s+1)$. The batch order\n",
    "  is replayed from the same RNG sequence, which the epoch-boundary check above confirms is right.\n",
    "* **`full`** — the gradient of the whole training loss at $W(s)$. Smoother, but not what Adam saw.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both are computed and cached; `WHICH_GRAD` picks which one the animation shows.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Colour scales\n",
    "\n",
    "Parameters use a **fixed** scale across the window (amplitude growth is the story) and gradients\n",
    "use a **per-frame** scale (magnitude spans orders of magnitude, so a fixed scale would go black).\n",
    "Each panel's title carries its own $|max|$ and $\\|\\cdot\\|_F$ so nothing is hidden by the choice.\n"
   ]
  },
